 "verdict": "**P — AND IT IS ONE ACT, NOT BOTH.** Tested against its OWN IPF constant-coupling null, **abortion's coupling MOVED** (spread **0.351** vs null 95th **0.159**, more than twice) and **homosexuality's did NOT** (spread **0.089**, INSIDE its null **0.153**).\n  Residual share for abortion runs **0.620 → 0.450 → 0.269 → 0.401** across 1991·1998·2008·2018 while homosexuality sits at **0.845 → 0.755 → 0.800 → 0.788**.\n  **The act did not change. The question format did not change. A fixed property cannot move — so for abortion, Δ is not a fixed property of either.**\n  ⇒ **the A/D decomposition is the wrong decomposition for at least one of the two acts**: the coupling between *how wrong I think it is* and *what I want done about it* tightened and then loosened, on a question whose wording never moved.\n  ⚠ **and the marginal-drift rival is dead on its own terms**: under Goodman–Kruskal γ, which responds to marginal change differently from ρ, the spread is **larger** (0.376), not smaller. Range restriction shrinks under γ; this grew.\n  ⚠ **What moved it is NOT established here.** Four waves. The party-gap description is printed above and deliberately untested.",
